.TH PROJ 1 "October 2026" "proj 0.8.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
proj \- manage project development environments with Lmod
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B proj
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B proj
is a metadata-driven project environment manager for Linux with Zsh or Bash.
It coordinates Lmod modules, optional micromamba, conda, or mamba
environments, project
variables, paths, aliases, and lifecycle hooks.
It does not replace Git, Lmod, or Python environment management.
.PP
Managed metadata is stored in
.IR ~/.proj/envs/ name /project.toml
and points to the actual project root.
A shared installation adds a group registry whose environments every group
member can activate.
The active project is local to the current shell.
.SH QUICK START
.nf
cd /path/to/atlas
proj create atlas
proj activate atlas
proj config project.description="Atlas development"
proj set-python-env atlas
proj module add compiler gcc/14
.fi
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "proj create " name " [--root " directory "] [--shared [--group " group "] [--group-writable | --private]] [--use " name "]..."
Register a project. The root defaults to the current directory. Creates a
valid starter document in the private registry,
prints its location, and leaves optional fields commented.
.B --shared
creates it in the shared registry instead; only its creator may change it
unless
.B --group-writable
is also given (directory 2775, document 664). With
.B --private
only the group can see and change it (2770 and 660).
.B --group
gives the environment to a group you are in instead of the registry's group; its
members must also be able to reach the registry. Members cannot remove
environments they do not own.
.TP
.BR "proj share " name " [--group " group "] [--group-writable | --private]"
Move one of your private environments to the shared registry: same name, same
document (byte for byte) and files, with the permissions
.B proj create --shared
gives (directory 2755 and document 644, or 2775 and 664 with
.BR --group-writable ,
or 2770 and 660 with
.BR --private ;
.B --group
names the group).
Refused when the shared registry already has that name. The environment active
in the current shell cannot be moved.
.TP
.BR "proj access " name " [--owner-only | --group-writable | --private]"
Show, or with a flag change, who can see and change a shared environment:
.B --owner-only
(everyone who reaches the registry reads it, only its creator changes it),
.B --group-writable
(its group changes it) or
.B --private
(only its group sees and changes it). Only its creator can change it; the group
stays. The permissions of all its files change together.
.TP
.BR "proj unshare " name " [--force]"
The reverse: move a shared environment back to your private registry. Only its
creator can do it, and it is refused while the environment is active in the
current shell or looks active in a process you can see (a program started from
a shell that had activated it; other people's processes are visible only to
root).
.B --force
ignores the processes. Either command writes the new copy beside its
destination and removes the old one last, so a failure leaves the environment
where it was.
.TP
.BR "proj list " "[--json] [--scope all|shared|private] [--under " dir "]..."
List registered project environments with their scope and owner (for a shared
environment, user:group). An environment whose project folder you cannot enter
counts with the ones you cannot read. A private
environment shadows a shared one with the same name. Invalid documents are
reported and skipped; shared environments you have no permission to read (limited
to another group) are left out and counted in one line.
.B --json
prints one read-only JSON document (schema number, then per environment its
name, scope, project folder, bundles, modules with pinned versions, library
bundles kept apart, the linked Python environment when there is one, and
last-change time, with counts of skipped and unreadable environments) for other programs; protected
libraries are recognised by the
.B library_prefixes
and
.B library_names
configuration keys and never listed with a version.
.B --scope shared
leaves out your private registry, and each
.B --under
keeps only environments whose project folder is inside that directory.
.TP
.B proj catalog
Print, as JSON, what a person may add to a login: the modules with their
versions and the Unix groups that gate them (and whether the caller is in
one), the curated suites and bundles with the names of the modules each
contains, and how many library modules exist (never which). See
.IR docs/machine-readable.md .
.TP
.BI "proj activate " name
Load the generated project Lmod module, activate the configured Python
environment, change to the project root, and run startup hooks.
This command requires the shell integration. Activating an environment owned
by someone else prints a notice: its hooks and aliases run as you.
.TP
.B proj deactivate
Reverse the activation performed in the current shell, including putting back
any module your login had loaded that the environment displaced (switch off with
.B restore_login_modules = false
in the
.B [behavior]
table of the configuration). This also works when
the environment was removed while active.
.TP
.BR "proj use " "[" name[/version] "]... [--env " name "] [--list]"
Use a library, suite, or tool in an environment (the active one, or
.IR --env )
without naming a category or an exact module. A name is tried as the exact
module name, then as
.BI pdk- name
and
.BI suite- name ;
more than one match, or none, lists the candidates and fails. The category
comes from the module's
.B whatis("Category: ...")
tag. A bare name stores the bare module name, so the module's default version
decides;
.I name/version
pins it. In the active environment the module is also loaded into the current
shell and remembered; in another environment it applies at the next
activation. A module that cannot coexist with one already used (another
version, or the same Lmod family) replaces it. A module may carry
.B whatis("Group: ...")
(a Unix group, several, or
.BR any );
without membership, proj refuses and tells you to ask an administrator. With no
names it shows what the environment uses;
.B --list
shows what you could use by category, plus the modules that need a group you
lack. With the shell integration
.B use
applies to the current shell immediately.
.TP
.BR "proj unuse " name... " [--env " name ]
Stop using modules: unload them from the current shell when the environment is
active, and forget them.
.TP
.BR "proj remove " name " [--yes] [--delete-files | --keep-files]"
Delete an environment's
.IR project.toml
after asking for confirmation. Other files in its directory and its generated
modulefile are listed, and deleted only if confirmed or with
.BR --delete-files ;
.B --yes
alone keeps them. The project root and Python environment are never touched.
The environment active in the current shell cannot be removed, and only its
owner can remove a shared environment. Without a terminal,
.B --yes
is required.
.TP
.B proj current
Print the active project name. Returns nonzero when none is active.
.TP
.B proj status
Display the active project and report module or Python environment drift.
.TP
.BR "proj info " "[--env " name "]"
Render effective project metadata. With no option, select the active project
environment. The Zsh integration uses the dots bordered panel when available.
With no environment active and none named, show an overview instead: the
registered environments followed by the available modules, as
.BR "proj avail" .
.TP
.BR "proj avail " "[" category | name "] [--json]"
List the modules Lmod can load, grouped by category with their versions (the
default is marked
.BR * )
and the environments that use them. The optional argument is a category
(compiler, toolchain, application, or project) or part of a module name. A
modulefile declares its category with
.BR "whatis(""Category: toolchain"")" ;
modules without a tag are listed as uncategorized. Modules generated by proj
for its own environments are not listed. Output is boxed and colored on a
terminal and plain when piped; NO_COLOR and PROJ_COLOR=always|never override
detection.
.TP
.BR "proj config " "[--env " name "] " key [ = value ]
Read or write metadata. Without
.I --env
the active project is selected. Values use TOML syntax; unquoted text is
treated as a string. Environment-affecting changes take effect after
reactivation.
.TP
.BR "proj set-python-env " "[--env " name "] [--manager " manager "] " environment
Validate and link an existing environment of
.BR micromamba ,
.BR conda ,
or
.BR mamba ,
by name or by absolute prefix path. The manager defaults to the project's,
then to the configured
.BR python_manager . A shared environment records the prefix so every member activates
the same Python environment.
.B proj
does not create the Python environment.
.TP
.BR "proj configmenu " "[--env " name "] [--setup]"
Edit an environment in a full-screen menu: fixed facts and editable settings
on the left, the resulting
.I project.toml
with unsaved lines marked on the right. Dialogs list live choices (Python
managers, environments, Lmod modules) with a Rescan button. Ctrl+S saves;
Escape closes a dialog unchanged, and on the main screen asks before
discarding unsaved changes, defaulting to staying.
.B --setup
installs the menu's private runtime (Textual) under
.IR PREFIX /libexec/menu ;
it needs network access.
.TP
.BR "proj python-env list " "[--manager " manager ]
List environments reported by the default or named manager.
.TP
.BR "proj module " "{list|add|remove} " category " [" module "]"
Manage module dependencies. Categories are
.BR project ,
.BR toolchain ,
.BR application ,
and
.BR compiler .
The active project is selected unless
.I --env
is supplied.
.TP
.B proj env list
List generated Lmod project modules and whether each cache entry is current.
.TP
.BI "proj env show " name
Show validated metadata and the effective generated environment.
.TP
.B proj doctor
Check shell integration, Lmod, the Python manager, site configuration, the shared
registry, metadata, and writable cache state.
.TP
.BR "proj init " "[--shell zsh|bash] [--rc " file "] [--reverse]"
Add a marked block that sources the shell integration to a startup file:
.I ~/.zshrc
or
.I ~/.bashrc
by default, detected from
.BR SHELL .
Rerunning replaces the block;
.B --reverse
removes it.
.TP
.B proj install
Idempotently link the command, manual, and TLDR page into the
.IR bin " and " share
directories beside the checkout (for example
.I /tools/bin
for
.IR /tools/proj ).
Existing conflicting paths are never overwritten. Use
.I install.sh
for a standalone installation.
.TP
.BR "proj upgrade " "[--check] [--yes] [--force] [--ref " ref ]
Reinstall proj from its source repository. Downloads the archive of the
repository and ref recorded in
.IR PREFIX /etc/source.toml
(overridden by
.BR --ref ", " PROJ_REPO ", and " PROJ_REF ),
compares versions, and, after asking (or with
.BR --yes ),
runs that archive's
.I install.sh
against this installation with its original mode and group. The site
configuration and shell startup files are left alone, no dependencies are
installed, and the previous version is kept in
.IR PREFIX /.previous .
.B --check
only reports, exiting 1 when an update is available.
.B --force
reinstalls the same version or downgrades. A git checkout cannot be upgraded
this way; use git. When the installation is not writable, run it with the full
path under sudo.
.TP
.B proj --version
Print the installed proj version.
.SH CONFIGURATION KEYS
Generic configuration supports:
.PP
.nf
project.description     project.root
project.shell           python.manager
python.environment      paths.prepend
hooks.startup           hooks.shutdown
modules.<category>      environment.<variable>
aliases.<name>
.fi
.PP
Variables in
.B [environment]
are restored to their previous value when the environment ends. The
.B environment_paths
tables add folders to a list variable such as PYTHONPATH, only when they exist,
and remove just those entries again.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
proj config project.description="Research tools"
proj config environment.PROJECT_MODE=development
proj config paths.prepend='["${project.root}/bin"]'
proj config aliases.project-root='cd ${project.root}'
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.proj/envs/ name /project.toml
Managed project metadata.
.TP
.IR PREFIX /etc/config.toml
Site configuration written by the installer:
.BR registry ,
.BR shared_registry ,
.BR group ,
.BR python_manager ,
.BR library_prefixes ,
and
.BR library_names .
.TP
.I ~/.config/proj/config.toml
Optional user configuration; overrides site keys.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/proj/modulefiles
Generated Lmod modulefile cache.
.TP
.IR PREFIX /shell/init.zsh ", " PREFIX /shell/init.bash
Shell integration that permits activation to modify the parent shell.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PROJ_ACTIVE
Name of the active project.
.TP
.B PROJ_HOME
Override the managed registry home. Defaults to
.IR ~/.proj .
.TP
.B PROJ_CONFIG
Override the user configuration file.
.TP
.B PROJ_SITE_CONFIG
Override the site configuration file.
.TP
.BR PROJ_SHARED_REGISTRY ", " PROJ_GROUP
Override the shared registry and its group.
.TP
.B PROJ_PYTHON_MANAGER
Override the default Python manager.
.TP
.B PROJ_PROMPT
.B off
stops the shell adapters from putting
.B [proj:\fIname\fB]
in front of the prompt while an environment is active. The variable
.B PROJ_PROMPT_SEGMENT
is still maintained (empty when nothing is active), for prompts that place it
themselves; if the prompt already contains it, nothing is inserted. A
framework that draws the badge from
.B PROJ_ACTIVE
(dots) takes precedence, and
.B NO_COLOR
makes the segment plain.
.TP
.B PROJ_PROJECT_ROOTS
Colon-separated additional metadata roots.
.TP
.B PROJ_CACHE_HOME
Override the cache directory.
.TP
.B PROJ_GLOBAL_BIN
Override the public command directory during installation and shell setup.
.TP
.B PROJ_GLOBAL_MAN
Override the public manual root during installation and shell setup.
.SH COMPLETION
The Bash adapter registers a completion function. The Zsh adapter adds its
completion directory to
.B fpath
and supports both earlier and later
.B compinit
ordering. Commands, project names, configuration keys, module categories, and
Python environments are completed. The everyday commands (activate, deactivate,
list, status, current, use, unuse, create, info) win a shared prefix, so
.B proj ac<Tab>
completes
.BR activate ;
type one more letter for
.B access
or
.BR avail . Metadata discovery is deferred until
completion is requested.
.PP
For module changes, completion proceeds from action to category to module.
The
.B add
action offers modules currently available through Lmod; the
.B remove
action offers modules configured in that category for the selected project.
Proj categories organize metadata and do not claim that Lmod provides a
universal category taxonomy.
.SH NOTES
Lmod remains authoritative for environment mutation and uses the
.B project
family to enforce one active project environment.
The project's Python manager remains authoritative for Python environments.
Task metadata may be displayed, but
.B proj run
is not implemented.
.PP
When dots logging functions are available, lifecycle messages use their
colored success, information, and error styles. Otherwise proj uses a
standalone Unicode fallback.
.PP
.B --project
is retained as a compatibility alias for
.BR --env .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. Status 1 is used for negative queries such as no active
project. Status 2 indicates invalid configuration, unavailable integration, or
unsafe state.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zsh (1),
.BR bash (1),
.BR module (1),
.BR micromamba (1),
.BR conda (1)
